{"id":"VHH__geo__voro_split","collection":"VHH","category":"geo","source_path":"VHH/geo/voro_split.json","root_names":["VHH::Core::Geometry::voro_split"],"counts":{"nodes":51,"connections":108,"values":51,"overloads":1},"documentation_summary":"This compound splits a mesh based on a voronoi pattern. Note. This does not split faces, it breaks up the mesh according it's existing structure. This means that the final result is heavily dependent on the mesh resolution. If you want a higher resolution mesh I would recoomend converting to volume and back, this compound works fast.","documentation_search":"voro split This compound splits a mesh based on a voronoi pattern. Note. This does not split faces, it breaks up the mesh according it's existing structure. This means that the final result is heavily dependent on the mesh resolution. If you want a higher resolution mesh I would recoomend converting to volume and back, this compound works fast. Input incoming mesh The Mesh you want to split. input points Custom points to control the voronoi result. Anything plugged into this port will override the settings below. triangulate mesh Perform a triangulation on the mesh before splitting. number of tiles When input points is empty, this is the number of tiles you wish to have in your result. tile seed Random seed for the point scattering. scatter mode Random or Bluenoise scattering. Output output mesh array An array of meshes after the split. Each tile is broken away from the original mesh and added to the output array.","port_names":["incoming_mesh","input_points","number_of_tiles","output_mesh_array","scatter_mode","tile_seed","triangulate_mesh"],"node_types_search":["Core::Array::array_bounds","Core::Array::array_is_empty","Core::Array::array_size","Core::Array::empty_array","Core::Array::find_all_in_array","Core::Array::first_in_array","Core::Array::get_from_array","Core::Array::sequence_array","Core::Array::sort_array_with_indices","Core::Conversion::scalar_to_vector3","Co